<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,viewport-fit=cover">
<title>Duty Border Studio</title>
<style>
/* The marking itself is NOT here. It is gen_duty_grid.MARK_CSS, emitted below exactly as the
   board emits it, because a studio with its own copy stops predicting the board the first time
   one of them is edited. What IS here is the studio's own chrome: the panel, the stage, and the
   labels no player will ever see. */
:root{--panel:#E5D8B9;--ink:#2A2320;--gold:%(gold)s}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;background:#12100d;color:#EDE6D6;
  font:14px/1.5 "Iowan Old Style","Palatino Linotype",Palatino,Georgia,serif}
header{padding:20px 24px 0}
h1{font-size:20px;margin:0 0 3px}
.sub0{color:#9FC49A;font-size:12.5px;margin:0;max-width:940px}
.wrap{display:flex;gap:22px;align-items:flex-start;padding:18px 24px 34px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.panel{background:var(--panel);color:var(--ink);border:1.35px solid var(--ink);border-radius:11px;
  padding:14px 16px 16px;width:clamp(280px,100%%,364px);flex:0 0 auto}
.row{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:10px;margin:12px 0}
.rlab{width:82px;font-weight:700;font-size:12.5px;padding-top:6px;flex:0 0 auto}
.opts{display:flex;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap;flex:1;min-width:0}
select{width:100%%;padding:6px 8px;border:1px solid #A89B7E;border-radius:7px;background:#EFE6CC;
  color:var(--ink);font:13px Georgia,serif}
button.opt{padding:5px 9px;cursor:pointer;background:#EFE6CC;border:1px solid #A89B7E;
  border-radius:7px;color:var(--ink);font:12px Georgia,serif}
button.opt:hover{background:#F6EFD9}
button.opt[aria-pressed=true]{border-color:var(--ink);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 2px var(--ink)}
input[type=range]{width:100%%}
.hint{font-size:11.5px;color:#5d5443;margin:9px 0 0;line-height:1.45}
.stage{flex:1 1 520px;min-width:300px;background:#1b1813;border-radius:12px;padding:10px;
  display:flex;justify-content:center}
/* The viewBox is square, so width IS height here: capping the width by the leftover viewport
   height is what keeps the whole wheel on screen instead of letting a wide window blow it up to
   its own width and push six tiles below the fold. */
svg{display:block;width:100%%;height:auto;max-width:min(100%%,calc(100dvh - 210px),760px)}
@supports not (height:100dvh){svg{max-width:min(100%%,calc(100vh - 210px),760px)}}
.fillp{fill:#272219}
.edge{fill:none;stroke:%(ink)s;stroke-opacity:.85;stroke-width:%(sw)s;stroke-linejoin:round}
.tname{fill:#8d8471;font:600 30px Georgia,serif;text-anchor:middle;pointer-events:none}
.tsub{fill:#6b6354;font:22px Georgia,serif;text-anchor:middle;pointer-events:none}
.cnt{fill:#c9bfa4;font:600 30px Georgia,serif;pointer-events:none}
.notes{padding:0 24px 40px;max-width:1000px}
.notes p{color:#a8a08c;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.6}
.notes b{color:#EDE6D6}
code{background:#241f18;padding:1px 5px;border-radius:3px;font-size:11.5px}
.warn{color:#e0a37a}
@media (max-width:760px){.panel{width:100%%}.stage{flex-basis:100%%}}
</style>
<style>%(markcss)s</style>
</head><body>
<header>
<h1>Duty Border Studio</h1>
<p class="sub0">The nine outlines are the real traced shapes from
<code>duty_grid_shapes.json</code>, at the board's own stroke width, over the ink edge the board
already draws. Every marking below is <code>gen_duty_grid.MARK_CSS</code>, the same rules the game
view runs.</p>
</header>
<div class="wrap">
<div class="panel">
  <div class="row"><div class="rlab">State</div><div class="opts">
    <select id="state"></select></div></div>
  <div class="row"><div class="rlab">Effect</div><div class="opts">
    <select id="anim"></select></div></div>
  <div class="row"><div class="rlab">Marked</div><div class="opts" id="which"></div></div>
  <div class="row"><div class="rlab">Speed</div><div class="opts" style="flex-direction:column">
    <input type="range" id="dur" min="500" max="4000" step="50" value="1800">
    <span class="hint" id="durlab"></span></div></div>
  <p class="hint" id="explain"></p>
  <p class="hint" id="support"></p>
</div>
<div class="stage">
<svg viewBox="0 0 %(box)d %(box)d" id="wheel" style="%(markvars)s;--dg-msw:%(sw)s">
<defs>%(markdefs)s</defs>%(body)s</svg>
</div>
</div>
<div class="notes">
<p><b>Gold is already spoken for.</b> The hover rule turns <code>.dg-edge</code>
<code>%(gold)s</code>, gated on <code>data-eligible</code>, and that is the only thing on the board
saying &ldquo;this is yours to take&rdquo;. A persistent mark wants a different <i>kind</i> of
signal, not a different shade &mdash; <code>%(fill)s</code> at hue 146&deg; is the one already
designed for it, and is what <code>MARK_FILL</code> now is.</p>
<p><b>The two dynamic-segment variants differ in one number.</b> Normalising to
<code>pathLength</code> means the segment count is what sets the segment LENGTH: at <i>n</i>
segments each lit run is about 1/<i>n</i> of the outline, whatever the waves are doing. So v2 is
%(seg2)d rather than %(seg)d, which halves every run &mdash; a mean lit length of about 29 of 1000
against the <code>ants</code> dash of %(ants)d, roughly twice it, where the %(seg)d-segment version
sits near 58.</p>
<p><b>Only one of these needs a script, and it is not an implementation detail.</b> CSS can
interpolate between two states; it cannot be a function of time, and the segment pattern is three
sine waves at rates sharing no common period, so it never comes back round. The loop is
<code>gen_duty_grid.MARK_JS</code> &mdash; the same one the game view runs &mdash; and it writes
<code>stroke-dasharray</code> inline, which beats the stylesheet. Left behind on the way out it
would override the array <code>ants</code> sets and corrupt every other option, visible only after
visiting this one first, so it clears both properties whenever the effect changes.</p>
<p><b>A border marks a whole tile, and %(twocount)d of the nine carry two actions.</b> Clipping a
closed outline to one half does not give you half a border, it gives you a cut one, and it reads as
damage. So if eligibility after a Sow is ever per action rather than per tile, the border can only
say &ldquo;something here&rdquo; &mdash; the half-art lighting the hover already uses
(<code>dg-lit-L</code> / <code>dg-lit-R</code>) is the thing that knows how to mean half a tile.</p>
<p><b>None of this survives the PNG path.</b> CSS animation is a browser thing; CairoSVG renders
the static state, the same renderer-dependence that got an SVG filter rejected for the serf
compositing. Keep frame zero legible on its own: <i>steady</i> survives it, <i>pulse</i> at
0&percnt; does not, and <i>portal</i> at frame zero is a dashed ring with the four dots stacked at
the path start.</p>
<p><b>Reduced motion is honoured in <code>MARK_CSS</code> itself</b>, not here &mdash; the page
cannot see those rules, so the component has to carry the media query. The segment loop checks the
same query separately, because it has no keyframes to suppress.</p>
</div>
<script>%(markjs)s</script>
<script>
const STATE = %(states)s;
const ANIMS = %(effects)s;
const WHICH = [['some','as listed'],['all','all nine'],['one','just one']];
const S = {state:'take', anim:'segmentsv2', which:'some', dur:1800};
const svg = document.getElementById('wheel');
const COUNTS = %(counts)s, ELIGIBLE = %(eligible)s;

function fillSelect(el,items,key){
  el.innerHTML = items.map(([v,l]) => `<option value="${v}">${l}</option>`).join('');
  el.value = S[key];
  el.onchange = () => { S[key] = el.value; apply(); };
}
function fillBtns(host,items,key){
  host.innerHTML = items.map(([v,l]) =>
    `<button class="opt" data-k="${key}" data-v="${v}">${l}</button>`).join('');
  host.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(b => b.onclick = () => { S[b.dataset.k]=b.dataset.v; apply(); });
}
fillSelect(document.getElementById('state'),
  Object.entries(STATE).map(([k,v]) => [k, v.label]), 'state');
fillSelect(document.getElementById('anim'), ANIMS.filter(e => e[0]), 'anim');
fillBtns(document.getElementById('which'), WHICH, 'which');

function apply(){
  const st = STATE[S.state];
  svg.setAttribute('data-mark-effect', S.anim);
  svg.style.setProperty('--dg-mc', st.colour);
  svg.style.setProperty('--dg-mdur', S.dur + 'ms');
  document.getElementById('durlab').textContent = S.dur + ' ms base cycle';
  document.getElementById('explain').innerHTML = st.note;
  [...svg.querySelectorAll('.dgt')].forEach(g => {
    const i = +g.dataset.i;
    let on = S.which === 'all' ? true : S.which === 'one' ? i === 1 : ELIGIBLE.includes(i);
    if(S.state !== 'take') on = COUNTS[i] > 0 && (S.which === 'one' ? i === 1 : true);
    if(on) g.setAttribute('data-mark','1'); else g.removeAttribute('data-mark');
    g.querySelector('.cnt').textContent =
      (S.state === 'take') ? '' : (COUNTS[i] ? COUNTS[i] + ' ●' : '');
  });
  document.querySelectorAll('button.opt').forEach(b =>
    b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', S[b.dataset.k] === b.dataset.v));
  if(window.dgMark) window.dgMark(svg, S.anim, S.dur);
}
// Say plainly whether this browser can do the dots, rather than showing nothing and looking broken.
const ok = CSS.supports('offset-path','path("M0 0 L1 1")');
document.getElementById('support').innerHTML = ok
  ? 'offset-path supported — the portal dots follow the real outline.'
  : '<span class="warn">This browser has no offset-path, so the portal’s dots cannot follow '
    + 'the outline and are hidden. The rings still run.</span>';
if(!ok) document.querySelectorAll('.dg-mdot').forEach(d => d.style.display = 'none');
apply();
</script></body></html>
